  "workflows": [
    {
      "key": "research",
      "name": "Research Agent",
      "description": "Give it a topic and an audience; the agent runs real web searches, opens the best sources, and streams back a cited markdown briefing. Differentiator: genuine multi-step tool use (search + browse) with inline citations — not a single prompt call.",
      "sourceId": "nodetool-base/Research Agent",
      "graph": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "id": "comment-intro",
            "parent_id": null,
            "type": "nodetool.workflows.base_node.Comment",
            "data": {
              "comment": "# 🔎 Research Agent\n\nType a topic, name the audience, and get back a sourced briefing. The agent breaks the topic into questions, runs Google searches, opens the best pages with the browser tool to verify details, then streams a markdown brief with inline [n] citations.\n\n**Graph:** topic + audience → compose objective → research agent (search + browse) → streaming brief\n\n**Why a graph, not a chat box:** the two inputs are composed into a structured research objective, and the agent's tool loop (search → browse → cross-check) runs on every Run with the same rigor. Swap the model or add tools without rewriting a prompt.\n\nNeeds an OpenAI key for the default model. Search + browse tools run against the open web."
            },
            "ui_properties": {
              "selected": false,
              "position": {
                "x": -480,
                "y": 40
              },
              "zIndex": 0,
              "width": 420,
              "height": 320,
              "selectable": true
            },
            "dynamic_properties": {},
            "dynamic_outputs": {}
          },
          {
            "id": "input-topic",
            "parent_id": null,
            "type": "nodetool.input.StringInput",
            "data": {
              "name": "topic",
              "value": "Small language models running on-device in 2026",
              "description": "What to research — a subject, question, or claim to investigate.",
              "line_mode": "multi_line"
            },
            "ui_properties": {
              "title": "1️⃣ Topic",
              "position": {
                "x": 40,
                "y": 60
              },
              "zIndex": 0,
              "width": 280,
              "selectable": true
            },
            "dynamic_properties": {},
            "dynamic_outputs": {}
          },
          {
            "id": "input-audience",
            "parent_id": null,
            "type": "nodetool.input.StringInput",
            "data": {
              "name": "audience",
              "value": "a technical product team deciding whether to adopt on-device models",
              "description": "Who the brief is for — sets the depth, framing, and what to emphasize.",
              "line_mode": "multi_line"
            },
            "ui_properties": {
              "title": "2️⃣ Audience",
              "position": {
                "x": 40,
                "y": 300
              },
              "zIndex": 0,
              "width": 280,
              "selectable": true
            },
            "dynamic_properties": {},
            "dynamic_outputs": {}
          },
          {
            "id": "compose-objective",
            "parent_id": null,
            "type": "nodetool.text.Prompt",
            "data": {
              "prompt": "Research the topic below and write a briefing document for the stated audience.\n\nTOPIC: {{ topic }}\nAUDIENCE: {{ audience }}\n\nMethod:\n1. Break the topic into 3–5 concrete research questions.\n2. Use google_search to find authoritative, recent sources for each question.\n3. Use the browser tool to open the most promising results and pull out specifics — facts, figures, dates, direct quotes. Do not rely on search snippets alone.\n4. Cross-check every claim against at least two sources before stating it as fact.\n\nWrite the answer as a single markdown document with:\n- A `#` title naming the topic.\n- A **TL;DR** section: 3–4 bullet takeaways written for the audience.\n- Themed `##` sections covering the key findings, in a logical order.\n- A final **Sources** section: a numbered list of every URL you actually opened, as markdown links.\n\nCitations:\n- Put an inline [n] marker after any specific claim, matching the numbered entry in Sources.\n- Tailor depth and framing to the AUDIENCE.\n- Keep the tone neutral and precise, no filler. If sources disagree, say so and note which you trust more and why."
            },
            "ui_properties": {
              "title": "3️⃣ Compose research objective",
              "position": {
                "x": 400,
                "y": 120
              },
              "zIndex": 0,
              "width": 340,
              "selectable": true
            },
            "dynamic_properties": {
              "topic": "Small language models running on-device in 2026",
              "audience": "a technical product team deciding whether to adopt on-device models"
            },
            "dynamic_outputs": {}
          },
          {
            "id": "research-agent",
            "parent_id": null,
            "type": "nodetool.agents.Agent",
            "data": {
              "model": {
                "type": "language_model",
                "provider": "openai",
                "id": "gpt-5-mini",
                "name": "GPT-5 mini",
                "path": null,
                "supported_tasks": []
              },
              "system": "You are a research analyst. You produce accurate, well-sourced briefings for a specific audience.\n\nYou have two tools:\n- google_search: find sources on the open web.\n- browser: open a URL and read its full content.\n\nMethod:\n- Search first, then open the best results with the browser to verify details — never state a fact from a search snippet alone.\n- Prefer primary sources and recent, reputable material.\n- Every factual claim must trace to a page you actually opened.\n- When evidence is thin or sources conflict, say so plainly instead of guessing.\n\nOutput: a single markdown document. Use inline [n] citation markers tied to a numbered Sources list. Write it as you go so the reader sees progress.",
              "prompt": "",
              "tools": [
                {
                  "type": "tool_name",
                  "name": "google_search"
                },
                {
                  "type": "tool_name",
                  "name": "browser"
                }
              ],
              "image": [],
              "audio": [],
              "history": [],
              "thread_id": "",
              "max_tokens": 100000
            },
            "ui_properties": {
